---
title: Your first C++ project
description: Create a project in Neovim, personalize its headers, add a module, and build, run, and test it.
section: Start here
order: 3
updated: '2026-10-13'
---

This C++26 walkthrough creates an application named `engine`, adds a small single-file module, and uses a compiler error to try the quickfix workflow. The result is an ordinary CMake project you can keep editing afterward. For another language, start with [installation and language selection](/docs/start-here/#choose-your-first-project).

## Load Rivet and check the tools

Use Neovim 0.11 or newer and [install Rivet through your plugin manager](/docs/start-here/#install-in-neovim). Restart after changing the configuration. Run `:RivetDoctor` to check the CMake/compiler tools used by this project. This walkthrough needs CMake 3.30+, Ninja 1.11+, and a C++26 compiler with named-module dependency scanning. On macOS, select Homebrew LLVM `clang++` and its matching `clang-scan-deps`; `clang-format` is optional. See [tool requirements](/docs/getting-started/#check-the-tools) for details.

## Create and personalize

In a parent directory of your choice, create a folder and open Neovim inside it:

```sh
mkdir engine
cd engine
nvim
```

Run `:pwd` to confirm the destination, then `:RivetNew` and use these choices. If a saved-profile menu appears, select **New choices** first:

| Prompt | Choice for this walkthrough |
| --- | --- |
| Language | `cpp` (C++) |
| Create project in | Current folder |
| Project name | `engine` (inferred from the folder, editable) |
| Git repository | Initialize Git (reuse an existing repository), or Skip Git initialization |
| Project license | No license yet, or your chosen license with its requested holder/year |
| File comment headers | Personalize and preview styles, or No header |
| C++ standard | C++26 |
| Workload profile | General C++ |
| Project kind | Application + reusable core |
| C++ interface | Named modules |
| Module files | Interface + implementation |
| Standard library | Standard-library headers |
| Project layout | Folder by feature |
| Formatting style | LLVM |
| Header protection | `#pragma once` |
| C++ compiler | Your module-capable `clang++` executable |

If you personalize headers, enter the details you want included; every field is optional. Keep `today` to stamp the creation date of each new file. Leave copyright and license fields blank unless you have text to include. The next panel compares the styles using your answers. Choose **Xcode-style** to try the spaced file-and-project header. The [header guide](/docs/comment-headers/) covers editing it later.

Ninja is selected automatically for named modules. The standard library still uses `#include`; `import std;` is a separate option and is not needed for this walkthrough.

Inspect the final file tree, Git action, and previews. Press `c` or Ctrl-S to create the project, or `q` to cancel. Everything is created directly in `engine`; no nested `engine/engine` folder is added. Enter selects options in ordinary pickers; the final review deliberately uses a separate apply key.

A C++ `.gitignore` is generated even if you skip Git initialization. Existing ignore rules are preserved; unrelated files stay in place, and collisions with other generated files stop creation. If Git is selected, an existing repository is reused or a new one initialized, with no staging or commit.

## Build, run, and test the starter

Open the generated application:

```vim
:edit app/main.cpp
:RivetTask build
```

Choose **debug** on the first task. Output appears as configure and build run. A failure stops the workflow; the output explains which step failed. A successful final step ends with `Exit status: 0`.

Run `:RivetTask run`. It configures and builds before launching the application, which prints:

```text
Worker 0: [0, 4)
```

Then run `:RivetTask test`. CTest runs `engine_test`; the starter should report one passing test. It checks the range-partitioning example and does not require an external test framework.

Use `q` to close task output. Rivet remembers Debug for this project across editor restarts, so subsequent tasks reuse it. `:RivetPreset` changes that choice; `:RivetTask repeat` repeats your last task with the currently selected preset and application. With more than one application, `:RivetRunTarget` changes which one Run launches.

## Add a single-file module

Run `:RivetFile`, select **C++** in the language picker, choose **Module interface**, select the `engine_core` target, use the name `number`, and name the module `engine.number`. The review should add `src/core/number.cppm` and register it in the core target's `CXX_MODULES` file set. Approve with `c`.

Open that file. Keep its optional comment header and replace the template below it with:

```cpp
export module engine.number;

export namespace engine {
constexpr int answer() {
  return 42;
}
}
```

This module includes both the exported declaration and the definition. It needs no separate `.cpp` implementation file. The generated core module uses a split interface and implementation to demonstrate that alternative; both arrangements are valid.

Now open `app/main.cpp`. Keep its optional comment header and replace the code below it with:

```cpp
#include <iostream>

import engine.core;
import engine.number;

int main() {
  std::cout << "Answer: " << engine::answer() << '\n';
  const auto range = engine::partition_work(10, 3, 0);
  std::cout << "Worker 0: [" << range.begin << ", " << range.end << ")\n";
  return 0;
}
```

Save both files with `:wall`, then run `:RivetTask run`. You should see `Answer: 42` followed by the original worker range. Rivet builds saved files, so save your edits before starting a task.

## Follow a compiler error

In `app/main.cpp`, temporarily change `engine::answer()` to `engine::answr()`, save, and run `:RivetTask build`. The build should fail and report the missing member.

Run `:copen`. Rivet collects GCC/Clang-style file diagnostics while output streams. Select an entry and press Enter to jump to its file and location; `:cnext` and `:cprevious` move between entries. Relative compiler paths resolve from the build directory.

Correct the typo, save, and run `:RivetTask repeat`. The build should succeed. Run `:RivetTask test` once more to check the original tests. A fresh workflow gets a fresh quickfix list, so old errors do not remain in the new result.

## Make the next change

Use `:RivetHeader` to preview an edit to the current file's comment header, or `:RivetHeaderDefaults` to change the defaults for future files. `:RivetCMake` opens the selected build's cache options; [CMake and tasks](/docs/cmake/) explains the controls.

For another component, use [Files and targets](/docs/templates/). Keep custom CMake logic in `CMakeLists.txt`; generated target and dependency fragments are maintained through Rivet's reviewed operations.

## Document the API with Doxygen

Choose **Doxygen documentation** while personalizing headers, or run `:RivetHeaderDefaults` before adding more files. The template picker shows a sample header; the final review shows the real filename and complete generated contents.

Add `@brief`, `@param`, and `@return` comments to exported declarations in `core.cppm`. Generate HTML for readers and XML for a documentation application using the [Doxygen guide](/docs/doxygen/). This lets the API reference grow with your first project and carry the same Git/release identity.
